{"type":"item.started","item":{"id":"item_1","type":"command_execution","command":"/bin/zsh -lc 'cat collab/1791190689957-architect-linker-m-next-d-plan.claude-fable-5-1.stdout.log'","aggregated_output":"","exit_code":null,"status":"in_progress"}}
{"type":"item.completed","item":{"id":"item_1","type":"command_execution","command":"/bin/zsh -lc 'cat collab/1791190689957-architect-linker-m-next-d-plan.claude-fable-5-1.stdout.log'","aggregated_output":"Completed-GMT: 2026-10-05 09:00:52 GMT\nCompleted-Local: 2026-10-05 16:00:52 +07\n\n**Summary: stage D makes the driver, not the engine, perform every stream effect of a task under custody, through one engine seam (observe/apply split plus a gate), a fenced writer, a recorded reader and a handoff driver that speaks only the C11 front; 12 slices, D1 to D12.**\n\nRead-only; nothing edited, no suites run. I read the brief, the landed authority namespaces' docstrings and signatures, the engine's put, park and wait-poll paths, `handoff.cljc`'s lift of pendings, and the REPL's step loop. I did not read the C12 reports, `yin-repl-design.md`, or the linker's sections 5.2 and 9 in full; where the plan leans on those it says so.\n\n## 0. What the tree says today\n\n- **The engine does the IO itself.** `handle-put` calls `stream/append!` at once (`engine.cljc` 480-503); `handle-next` and `handle-poll` read at once; `check-wait-set` retries appends and reads on every sweep (1592-1651). Nothing assigns an id, records an input, or asks an authority.\n- **No exporting state exists in the handoff.** `export-task` is a pure lift; a grep of `handoff.cljc` for an export record, a detach or an abort finds none. UCF 7.7.4's transition is still owed.\n- **`handoff-version` is 0.** Export encodes with `dao.stream.cbor` (line 719) and addresses with `jing/content-hash` (line 725).\n- **The authority side is complete as plain functions.** Holders reach it through `authority.front` (requests `:yin.k/offer`, `:yin.k/proposal`, `:yin.k/resumed`, `:yin.k/release`, `:yin.k/input`, `:yin.k/admit`; replies carry `:yin.k/request-id`). Committed outcomes are also on `admission/outcome-reader`. `custody/binding-evidence`, `front/reply-evidence`, `input/frontier`, `input/inputs` and `input/replay-input` are the reader-side helpers. Input source kinds are closed: read, FFI result, link result.\n- **`yin.repl.core` no longer exists.** The composition root is `yin.repl.main/step-all`, the single step owner, over the `yin.repl` shell, `yin.repl.driver`, `yin.repl.link` and `yin.repl.dht`.\n- **C12 is on branch `ucf-c12-gate`, not yet on master.** `authority/durability` and `exclusive-capable?` are not in master's `authority.cljc`. D11 depends on C12 landing.\n\n## 1. The contract\n\n### 1.1 A task under custody\n\nA version-1 task carries one plain-data map on its root machine value, `:yin.k/custody`:\n\n- the occurrence its grant names, the lease, the epoch from the binding, the arbitration identity;\n- `:yin.k/next-op-seq`;\n- the input state: next `k`, the lease's frontier, and whether the replay prefix has ended;\n- the protection class of each reachable stream identity (1.5);\n- a run state: `:running`, `:ended` (after `:stale`, `:intent-conflict`, replay divergence, or the lease bound).\n\nInstall children carry none of this. They are found through the root and draw from its counters. A version-0 task has no such map and behaves exactly as today.\n\n### 1.2 The engine seam (the one engine change)\n\nEvery place the engine touches a stream is split into two functions:\n\n- **observe:** handle IO, returning an outcome as data;\n- **apply:** outcome to machine value (wake, park, convert a retained request into a sent one, advance a cell).\n\nToday's behavior is `apply ∘ observe`, unchanged, and the existing parity suites are the proof.\n\nThe gate: when the root has `:yin.k/custody`, the engine never observes for that task or its install children. An immediate effect parks as if the outcome were `full` or `blocked`. `check-wait-set` skips gated entries, as it already skips link and FFI-response entries. The driver observes, then calls the public `apply`.\n\nThis covers every pending path the brief names: `:stream/put`, `:stream/next`, the FFI request append and its response routing, the link request and its response, and the stepping of install children's entries.\n\nImmediate effects with no wait variant:\n- **`:stream/poll` and `:stream/cursor`** are observations. Under custody they are held for one driver step, recorded as `:yin.k/read` inputs (a poll's `blocked` and a minted cursor are the observed values), then applied.\n- **`:stream/close`** on an enrolled target answers the shaped refusal value; closing a target is the authority's act. On an unenrolled stream the driver performs it.\n- **`:stream/make`** creates a task-local stream; its class is the composition's declared default (1.5).\n- **Lift waits for held immediates.** A task with a held poll or cursor entry is not at a liftable safepoint; export refuses `:yin.k/non-portable`, kind `:reason-mismatch`, until the driver has applied it. No pending variant is added to the wire.\n\n### 1.3 The fenced writer\n\nPure functions over the machine value and data; the driver does the appends.\n\n1. **Assign.** For a retained write whose target is enrolled and which has no id: take `n`, store `{:yin.k/occurrence P :yin.k/seq n}` on the entry, set the counter to `n+1`. One step, before any request is sent. At 2^52-1 nothing is assigned and nothing sent.\n2. **Send.** Build the five-key envelope from the entry and the custody map, and append an `:yin.k/admit` request to the holder's inbound stream. The request id is derived from the lease and the op id, so a retry is the same request.\n3. **Retain.** The id stays through a `full` inbound stream, an append of unknown effect, no outcome yet, and `:suspended`.\n4. **Discharge.** Only an outcome that passes `front/reply-evidence`, and matches the op id and the incarnation, counts. It is read from the reply stream or from the outcome projection at a kept cursor.\n   - `:committed` and `:replayed`: apply the recorded result to the entry. `ok` wakes a `:put`, turns an `:ffi-request` into a sent `:ffi`, and a `:link-request` into a `:link-response`. A recorded `closed` applies as `closed`.\n   - `:suspended`: nothing; retry.\n   - `:stale` and `:intent-conflict`: the run ends. No further fenced emission, no successor. On a conflict the driver still releases, so the judge does not wait out silence.\n5. **A duplicate outcome** for a discharged wait is skipped.\n\nA diagnostic is never an outcome. An authority that cannot be reached yields no outcome; the wait stays and the driver retries.\n\n### 1.4 The recorded reader\n\n- **Live:** observe the handle, send `:yin.k/input` with the source and the observed outcome (value, successor cursor, `end`, or `gap` with its successor), and apply only after `:recorded` or `:replayed`.\n- **Replay:** while `k` is below the frontier the driver observes nothing live. It takes record `k` and applies it to the waiting entry whose source it names. If the task is quiescent and no entry matches, the run ends as a replay divergence and the driver releases.\n- **Sources:** a read is named by stream identity and cell; an FFI result by call id; a link result by link id. Because replay is directed by source, the order across root and children is the recorded order, whatever order the driver would have polled.\n- **End of prefix:** at `k` equal to the frontier, live observation resumes and is recorded from there.\n\n### 1.5 Protection classes\n\nThe composition declares, per stream identity, one of:\n\n- `:enrolled`: writes go through the fenced writer.\n- `:at-least-once`: the driver appends bare, with no id; a regrant may repeat it.\n- `:fail-stop`: a write under custody ends the run.\n\nAn undeclared stream refuses activation and export as `:yin.k/unsatisfied` naming the stream. Enrollment is read from the arbitration ledger by both ends; a declaration of `:enrolled` the ledger does not confirm is the same refusal. Reads are recorded whatever the class.\n\n### 1.6 The version-1 wire\n\n- **Bytes and address:** `jing/canonical-bytes` of the body; the address is the segment address of those bytes.\n- **Reader:** `handoff-version` becomes the set `#{0 1}`. Validation runs `checkpoint/inspect` first (one implementation of the custody grammar), then the restoration grammar: code, cells, registers, clause 5.\n- **Lift:** adds the custody header, carries each id on its pending, carries phase and parent on install entries, and refuses `:unprotected-pending` and `:op-seq-exhausted`.\n- **Lower:** restores the counter exactly, installs the custody map only from a grant with valid binding evidence, and checks protection in both directions.\n- **Determinism:** lift is a pure function of the export record. Cell ids and value-table order come from a fixed traversal, so two lifts of one record, on any host, give the same bytes and so the same baseline digests.\n- **Version 0 is frozen.** Its three defects are fixed as version-0 behavior with version-0 tests.\n\n### 1.7 The handoff driver\n\nOne explicit step state, plain data, stepped by the composition. It reaches the authority only through the front's streams, in process or remote.\n\n**Source (first export, or a holder at its next safepoint):**\n1. Enter exporting (7.7.4): require an empty ready queue, move the wait set and reachable parked records into the export record, refuse local resume.\n2. Mint the occurrence once and persist it.\n3. Lift, append code and body to the carrier, then send the offer.\n4. A holder exiting then sends the resumed report and the release. A halt sends its result body the same way.\n5. Retry each phase from retained progress; never mint a second occurrence for one park.\n\n**Abort** is legal before any offer request was appended, or on an authority-attributed refusal of the offer. Otherwise the source stays fenced and resends the offer, which is idempotent; after an admitted offer the only way back is a grant to itself.\n\n**Candidate:**\n1. Fetch the bytes; validate fully with zero side effects.\n2. Check the authority attachment; missing is `:yin.k/unsatisfied`.\n3. Propose. Unanswered is `:yin.k/awaiting-grant`; a grant to another is `:yin.k/not-holder`.\n4. Accept only a grant with `binding-evidence`. An invalid binding is `:yin.k/not-holder`, followed by a release.\n5. Read the frontier and inputs, lower into a private task, run under the gate.\n6. Any failure after the grant releases first; a pending release is retried.\n\n**Liveness:** the driver renews through `dao.lease`'s holder functions on its own tick stream, and stops all IO at the bound.\n\n**Progress:** the driver appends its progress records (occurrence, publication, offer, proposal, grant, release) to a `dao.stream.journal` the composition supplies, and reopens by folding it.\n\n### 1.8 Composition and the REPL\n\n- `yin.vm.ucf.compose` holds plain functions: an authority side (open, judge step, one front step per holder, target and outcome readers) and a holder side (the driver step). Nothing in it requires `yin.repl`.\n- Exclusive is offered only when `authority/exclusive-capable?` holds for the composition's required failure model. Otherwise the composition offers fork and labels it fork.\n- The carrier for bodies and code is the node's existing content store and the landed staged module load. No second loader; the DHT node stays the only DHT step owner.\n- The REPL wiring is one more step inside `yin.repl.main/step-all`, after the shell step, plus shell commands to export, resume and show custody state.\n\n## 2. Slices\n\nAll code is `.cljc`. Each slice runs JVM during iteration and the three lanes once at landing. Paths are under `src/cljc/` unless they begin with `test/`.\n\n| Slice | Scope | Files | 7.11.1 evidence | Depends on |\n|---|---|---|---|---|\n| D1 | The three version-0 defects, each with a version-0 test | `yin/vm/ucf/handoff.cljc`; `test/yin/vm/ucf/handoff_test.cljc` | 5 (version-0 half) | none |\n| D2 | Engine observe/apply split and the custody gate | `yin/vm/engine.cljc`; new `test/yin/vm/engine_gate_test.cljc` | substrate | none |\n| D3 | Version-1 reader: gate, header, carried ids, clause 5, inspector as first stage | `handoff.cljc`; `test/yin/vm/ucf/handoff_v1_test.cljc` | 1, 2, 4 (structural), 5 | none |\n| D4 | Version-1 lift: header, ids, phase and parent, the two new refusals, determinism, fixture regeneration | `handoff.cljc`; `test/yin/vm/ucf/checkpoint_fixtures.cljc`, `checkpoint-v1.txt` | 4 (first exclusive export), 9 (sequence at lift) | D1, D3 |\n| D5 | Version-1 lower: counter restore, custody map, protection match | `handoff.cljc` | 3 (restoration), 4 (protection mismatch) | D2, D3 |\n| D6 | Fenced writer | new `yin/vm/ucf/holder/writer.cljc` | 3 (assignment), 6 (writer), 8 (driver), 9 (sequence) | D2 |\n| D7 | Recorded reader and replay | new `yin/vm/ucf/holder/reader.cljc` | 3 (delivery discipline) | D2 |\n| D8 | Exporting state, export record, abort | new `yin/vm/ucf/holder/export.cljc` | 7.11 exporting-state blocker | D2 |\n| D9 | Driver, candidate half | new `yin/vm/ucf/holder/driver.cljc` | 7 (holder) | D5, D6, D7 |\n| D10 | Driver, source and exit half, progress journal | `holder/driver.cljc` | 14.2.4 rows 2 and 7 at driver level | D4, D8, D9 |\n| D11 | Plain composition, protection declarations, exclusive gate; REPL wiring | new `yin/vm/ucf/compose.cljc`; `yin/repl.cljc`, `yin/repl/main.cljc` | 7.11 authority grounding and wiring | D10, C12 |\n| D12 | Safepoint harness on both versions; 14.2.4 rows through the plain composition; the stage-D gate | tests | safepoint block, 14.2.4 | D11 |\n\n**Order and concurrency.** D1, D2 and D3 start together. Then D4 and D5 (both edit `handoff.cljc`; run them in sequence, D5 first if D2 lands first). D6, D7 and D8 run concurrently after D2, in separate namespaces. Then D9, D10, D11, D12 in order.\n\n### D1, version-0 defects\n\n- A `:parked` body with frames restores those waits in order.\n- `validate-body` requires an entry for every `:install` pending, `:yin.k/undecodable`.\n- The returned address is the digest of the bytes export actually emits. The body's bytes do not change. The test asserts address and bytes agree on each host.\n\n### D2, engine seam\n\n- Split every observe from its apply; expose the applies.\n- Add the gate and the held-immediate rule of 1.2.\n- **Tests:** the full existing engine, FFI, link and handoff suites pass unchanged (no custody map means no behavior change). With a custody map, a task run against counting handles makes zero handle calls across put, next, poll, cursor, FFI call, link require, and an install child; each parks. Applying a supplied outcome produces the same machine value as the ungated path given that outcome.\n\n### D3, version-1 reader\n\n- Fixtures on canonical bytes, per clause: version 2, absent, float `1.0`, a version-0 child in a version-1 root; each missing header key, fork policy, nil occurrence, origin equal to self, header keys on a child, halted root and halted child forms; an id on `:next`, at or above the counter, duplicated, without an origin, naming the body's own occurrence.\n- Clause 5 on both versions: `:park` and `:call-effect` reasons, missing install entry, phase outside `:running` and `:parked`, each phase with each child kind, a runnable child, phase and parent required under version 1, halted forbids frames.\n- Assert zero attach calls, zero proposals and no machine on every refusal.\n- A reader that speaks both lowers a version-0 body as a fork and refuses it when exclusive is required.\n\n### D4, version-1 lift\n\n- Header on blocked and parked roots, origin only on a halted root, none on children.\n- `:unprotected-pending` for a first exclusive export over an attempted write to an enrolled stream; `:op-seq-exhausted` at 2^52-1.\n- Two lifts of one export record give equal addresses; the accepted C4 fixtures are regenerated through this lift and their pinned addresses change in this commit. The refusal fixtures stay mutations of those bases.\n- No envelope key appears anywhere in a body.\n\n### D5, version-1 lower\n\n- The counter is set to the carried value exactly; carried ids survive on `:put`, `:ffi-request` and `:link-request`.\n- Protection mismatch in each direction is `:yin.k/unsatisfied` naming the stream.\n- A restored child continues from its saved state without rerunning initialization or replaying its link request.\n- Different receiver-local store bindings do not change resolution (14.2.4 row 8).\n\n### D6, fenced writer\n\n- Assign-before-send, one increment, children drawing from the root.\n- Retention across `full`, unknown effect, no outcome, `:suspended`.\n- Each of the five outcomes; a forged `:committed` and a forged `:intent-conflict` discharge nothing; an outcome for another incarnation is ignored; a duplicate is skipped.\n- Unknown-effect transport error cut before and after the commit: one commit, or a replay of the result held.\n- The nested envelope-shaped program value arrives as payload.\n\n### D7, recorded reader\n\n- No input is applied before its acknowledgment (counted).\n- Regrant with a frontier of 3: three replays, zero live observations, then live.\n- Evicted value: with records the old intent and result are reproduced; without them the run fails closed; a divergent intent at the same id is `:intent-conflict` (14.2.4 row 5).\n- Root and child inputs interleaved; replay follows the recorded order.\n\n### D8, exporting\n\n- After entering exporting, polling the source, a direct resume, and ticking its install child append nothing (the blocked-writer hazard of 7.7.4).\n- Abort reinstates waits and parked records exactly.\n- A non-empty ready queue refuses `:yin.k/not-quiescent`.\n\n### D9, candidate\n\n- Two candidates over two encodings of one occurrence: one activates, the other gets `:yin.k/awaiting-grant` then `:yin.k/not-holder` (14.2.4 row 1, same host).\n- A binding by another author, in another record, duplicated, or with a float epoch: `:yin.k/not-holder` and a release.\n- Post-grant attachment failure releases, or retains release progress.\n- The source itself resumes only after its own grant.\n\n### D10, source and exit\n\n- Each carrier and offer append failed in turn, unknown acceptance included; the driver reopened after each phase: one occurrence, retained waits and ids, no source effect, no second grant.\n- Abort only before a proven unadmitted offer.\n- Exit cuts: after the successor append, the resumed report, the release append and closure. The driver resends idempotently; it never publishes a second successor.\n- A halt completes with a result body.\n\n### D11, composition and REPL\n\n- The plain composition runs a whole handoff in one process on the memory seam, with no `yin.repl` on the classpath of that test.\n- Without an exclusive-capable authority it offers fork and says so.\n- REPL: the custody step is inside `step-all`; no new thread, timer or step owner.\n\n### D12, harness and gate\n\n- Every corpus segment runs to each row of the 7.4.1 table, lifts and lowers into a fresh VM on both body versions, and matches the reference result and effect trace, including `lift(lower(frame))`.\n- 14.2.4 rows 1 to 5, 7 and 8 through the plain composition, same host, three lanes. Row 6's driver logic (suspended retries, no inference from absent lapse) is unit-tested here; the partition itself is E.\n\n## 3. Boundary with E\n\n- **D owns:** everything above, tested in one process per host over the memory seam and the file-backed authority.\n- **E owns:** the nine ordered host pairs through the wired composition; separate processes on JVM and Node and isolated runtimes on Dart; real partitions and process kills; clause 10 and the composition halves of clauses 3 and 8; the per-host durability record.\n- **D hands E:** `yin.vm.ucf.compose` as the only entry point E drives, the regenerated byte fixtures, and the driver's progress-journal format.\n\n## 4. Risks, riskiest first\n\n1. **D2, the engine split.** It touches every IO path of a 2,254-line engine. A missed path is an unfenced effect. The zero-handle-call test over every effect kind is the control.\n2. **D7, read gating cost and coverage.** Every read under custody becomes a durable authority transition. That is one fsync per input, and it may be too slow for chatty tasks. It is the price of the replay guarantee; say so rather than weaken it.\n3. **D4, deterministic encoding.** If lift depends on map iteration order anywhere, variants conflict at the authority and hosts disagree on fixtures.\n4. **D10, the abort rule.** An unknown offer append must leave the source fenced; a wrong early abort creates two runners.\n5. **D11, REPL shape.** The UCF and linker text name `yin.repl.core`, which is gone. The wiring target above is my reading of the current tree, not of `yin-repl-design.md`.\n6. **C12 not yet on master.** D11 cannot gate on `exclusive-capable?` until it lands.\n\n## 5. Document amendments implementation will force (listed, not written)\n\nCarried from the C4 rulings, still unplaced:\n- **UCF 7.2.1:** name `jing/canonical-bytes` and the segment-address form; a halt with no origin is a version-0 result; the emitter is required in an origin.\n- **UCF 7.2.1 version gate:** the statuses for a nested body (a version-0 child is `:yin.k/undecodable`; an unpublished child version is `:yin.k/profile-mismatch` with a path).\n- **UCF 7.4.3:** the entry-for-every-install rule is also a custody-baseline rule.\n- **UCF 7.7.8 *Snapshot variants*:** comparison is on digests of the carried encoding.\n- **UCF 7.9:** `:yin.k/hash-mismatch` covers a body's own address.\n- **Linker-dht 14.3:** the third version-0 defect.\n\nNew with D:\n- **UCF 7.7.4:** the exporting state as built, and the abort rule in terms of offer evidence.\n- **UCF 7.7.5 and 7.7.8:** the engine gate (the driver performs every effect of a task under custody); the three protection classes; held immediates (`poll`, `cursor`) as recorded reads; `close` on an enrolled target.\n- **UCF 7.7.8 *Operation sequence state*:** the request id derived from lease and op id.\n- **UCF 7.4.1 and 7.4.3:** a task with a held immediate is not at a liftable safepoint.\n- **UCF 7.9:** the driver's behavior after `:intent-conflict` includes a release; replay divergence ends a run.\n- **UCF 7.5.3:** the fixed traversal that makes lift deterministic.\n- **UCF 7.11 and linker-dht 14.3 item 4:** replace `yin.repl.core` with the current composition root.\n- **Linker-dht 14.2.2:** the driver's progress journal and its record kinds; source-directed replay.\n- **`yin.vm.ucf-revisions.md` section 6:** version 1 moves from published design to implemented, slice by slice.\n- **`yin.vm.semantic.md` or the engine's state doc:** the observe/apply split and the `:yin.k/custody` key on a machine value.\n","exit_code":0,"status":"completed"}}
{"type":"item.started","item":{"id":"item_2","type":"command_execution","command":"/bin/zsh -lc \"cat collab/1791125400000-reviewer-c12-gate-gate.glm.findings.md; rg -n '\"'^'\"\\\\(defn|request-id|frontier|source|read|replay' src/cljc/yin/vm/ucf/authority/input.cljc | head -65; rg -n '\"'^'\"\\\\(defn|request-id|retry|cached' src/cljc/yin/vm/ucf/authority/front.cljc | tail -45; rg -n 'stream/|waitset/|vm/run|custody' src/cljc/yin/vm/engine.cljc | tail -65; rg -n 'step-all|defn.*step' src/cljc/yin/repl/main.cljc; rg --files src | rg 'yin/repl/core'\"","aggregated_output":"","exit_code":null,"status":"in_progress"}}
{"type":"item.completed","item":{"id":"item_2","type":"command_execution","command":"/bin/zsh -lc \"cat collab/1791125400000-reviewer-c12-gate-gate.glm.findings.md; rg -n '\"'^'\"\\\\(defn|request-id|frontier|source|read|replay' src/cljc/yin/vm/ucf/authority/input.cljc | head -65; rg -n '\"'^'\"\\\\(defn|request-id|retry|cached' src/cljc/yin/vm/ucf/authority/front.cljc | tail -45; rg -n 'stream/|waitset/|vm/run|custody' src/cljc/yin/vm/engine.cljc | tail -65; rg -n 'step-all|defn.*step' src/cljc/yin/repl/main.cljc; rg --files src | rg 'yin/repl/core'\"","aggregated_output":"Completed-GMT: 2026-10-05 08:50:12 GMT\nCompleted-Local: 2026-10-05 15:50:12 +07\n\n# Gate review, M-next C slice C12: the stage-C gate (durability predicate, crash-cut matrix, cross-host ledger fixture)\n\n**VERDICT: APPROVE-WITH-NITS.** The substrate claim is proven by inspection: every transition under every journal crash cut reopens to exactly its pre-state or its post-state, the journal never holds a partial transaction, retries converge to the twin's post-state with the answers the landed slices document, and one fixed ledger's frames are byte-deterministic by construction with the digest computed host-independently. The one src edit is additive and correct. No landed-slice defect surfaced. The six findings below are one minor redundancy (`:clean?` as implemented is indistinguishable from \"not poisoned\" — the engineer's own mutation table is the evidence), one minor doc debt (`:persisted` semantics, owed since the C2 review), and four nits. Nothing blocks landing; the three-host lane run the orchestrator is executing remains the first live proof of the Node/Dart legs, everything there being JVM-verified today.\n\n## What I verified (read-only; no suite run, per the brief)\n\n**Attack 1, durability.** `exclusive-capable?` (`src/cljc/yin/vm/ucf/authority.cljc:189-210`) requires all of: `required` ∈ #{:process-crash :power-loss}; backend `:file`; lock kind ∈ #{:os-lock :claim-file} (so `:none` and missing are out); `:clean?`, not poisoned, not closed; and `(failure-rank required) <= (failure-rank declared)` with the rank `:none 0 < :process-crash 1 < :power-loss 2` and an undeclared model defaulting to 0 — so `:power-loss` covers `:process-crash`, never the reverse, and every host answers false for `:power-loss` because `file/durability` (`journal/file.cljc:29-41`) declares `:process-crash` everywhere. The memory declaration (`journal.cljc:99-106`, backend `:memory`, model `:none`, lock `:none`) can never pass the `:file` test — memory is never capable, as the plan demands. The declaration is read exactly once, at open! (`authority.cljc:140-141`, a thunk evaluated into the authority value), stays readable after poison and close (`durability_test.cljc:96-102`), and nil for a backend without the key — nil-safe in the predicate and pinned by `a-backend-without-a-declaration-is-not-capable`. Flag paths: `:clean?` is set only when `open!` mints the authority value (a refused open mints no value at all, so \"opened with no refusal\" holds of every authority by construction); `poison!` clears it (`authority.cljc:219`); `close!` leaves it set but the `:closed?` check answers false — and `grant/reopen!` goes through `open!` and *closes* the authority when a reclaim does not commit (`grant.cljc:388`), so that path fails the closed check exactly as the engineer claims. Forgery/staleness: the state atom is reachable by whoever holds the authority value, but that holder is the composition itself — the same trust it already needs not to rewrite `:projection`; the declaration is frozen at open from constants, so it cannot go stale. The honest limit is finding 2: the predicate never probes the live lock. Tests cover every arm the brief listed, including the per-host lock kind via reader conditional (`durability_test.cljc:83-85`) and poison through a cut wrapped around the *real* file seam (`:131-145`).\n\n**Attack 2, the crash-cut matrix.** All 11 brief-named transitions are rows (`crash_cut_test.cljc:129-214`) — enroll, offer, grant (judge step), reclaim, completion, halted completion, admit committed, admit intent-conflict, input record, close-target, refusal pair — each run under all three named cuts on memory, plus the 4-row file subset (reclaim, completion, admit committed, input record). The pre/post states come from an uncut twin built on a separate world (`twin`, `:285-299`) — independent of the cut run, as required — and the twin also asserts the expected `:fresh`/`:replay` answers and `pre ≠ post`, so no row runs vacuously and a wrong expected answer fails loudly; the cut leg further requires *poisoning* (answer in #{:suspended transport-error} **and** projection nil), which a non-committing transition cannot fake. Check (a) is the strong form: membership in {pre, post} **and** equality with the exact state the cut implies (`:332-338`). Check (c) folds the persisted frames record by record through the real ledger fold (`whole-transactions`, `:241-258`) and compares frame count and decoded frames against the twin's. Check (b) runs the same request twice — plain `open!` retry (converges to the twin's post) and `grant/reopen!` over a copy (the documented post-reclaim answers, `:347-354`) — with every expected answer traceable to a landed slice test (the C6/C7/C8/C10 citations in the engineer findings check out against the code: release-after-policy-reclaim → `invalid-value` via `lapse-decision`'s duplicate-lapse arm; quarantined admission → `:suspended` before tenure; reclaimed-lease admissions and inputs → `:stale` via the tenure checks). The judge row's lease renaming (`canon`, `:226-233`) hides nothing real: it replaces only the judge-minted random lease-id spelling by its grant's `t`, which is the journal position and therefore deterministic; holder, duration, proposal, epochs and every other byte are compared exactly, and a grant landing at a different t would rename differently and fail. The grant-t keying is also why the row is excluded from the file subset, letting that leg compare **raw** against the memory twin (fixed lease ids) — an honest strengthening, not a dodge. The file subset is real: `cut-backend` wraps the genuine `write-frame!` (skip / write / write-then-truncate-3-bytes, each followed by a throw), the handle is dropped via `fs/unlock!` as a dead process would leave it, and the torn tail is dropped by `dao.jing.file`'s own recovery before the journal reads — the C2-verified mechanism. The engineer's four purpose-breaks (atomicity/poison/replay/attribute-order) each name the failing tests and counts; consistent with what the code enforces.\n\n**Attack 3, the cross-host fixture.** `ledger-v1.txt` is 22 lines / 32046 bytes / pure ASCII: `ledger-v1`, `projection <blake3>`, then 20 frames = header + 19 transactions, matching next-t 19. The script (`ledger_fixtures.cljc:194-221`) exercises all **16** kinds of `ledger/attribute-order` — enrolled, target-closed, admitted, offered, accepted, bound, lapsed, reclaimed, rejected, refused, fenced, quarantined, input, resumed, completed, succeeded — with `:yin.k/succeeded` in both forms (continuation edge with `:yin.k/successor`, terminal edge with `:yin.k/result`), and `the-ledger-holds-every-fact-kind` asserts set equality with `(keys attribute-order)`, so a missing kind cannot pass. Digest host-independence: `digest` is BLAKE3 of `cbor/encode` of the projection; the canonical codec sorts map keys by their bytes (`cbor/boring.cljc:103-107`) and sets are tag 258 over elements sorted by canonical bytes (`cbor.cljc:1061-1069`), so host map/set iteration order never reaches bytes; `:admitted` is keyed by `cbor/content-key` (documented portable); every counter in the ledger is a small exact integer; nothing in the script mints a float. Randomness: none reaches bytes — the header is pre-seeded with the fixed identity `\"arb-c12\"` so `journal/open!`'s `random-uuid` arm (empty backend) never runs; lease ids are fixed strings; occurrence ids are fixed UUID strings; grants go through the writer, never the hook, so `mint-lease-id` is unreachable; time exists only as judge ticks, which the ledger never records. The tests can fail on divergence three ways: render equality of the whole file including the digest line (`ledger_fixture_test.cljc:69-70`), per-frame hex of this host's rebuild (`:77-82`), and the pinned digest over both a memory backend and a file backend the frames are replayed into, closed and reopened (`:98-125`). Nothing checked in produces the file: `ledger_fixtures.cljc` contains no write marker from the conformance guard's list (I re-ran the guard's marker set against it), no `spit` anywhere, the one-off probe namespace is gone from the tree, and the guard's scan itself covers the file.\n\n**Attack 4, scope and hygiene.** `git diff` touches exactly `authority.cljc` (+52: docstring sentences, the `:durability` capture, the `:clean?` init, one added key in `poison!`, `durability`, `failure-rank`, `exclusive-capable?`) — no slice semantic changed: `poison!` still poisons exactly as before, `close!`/`projection`/`transition!` untouched. No `yin.vm` engine namespace in any new require (only `yin.vm.ucf.*`); the test seam (`authority.seam/commit-effect!`) is not used; no `with-redefs`. No float literals in the five files; every reader conditional is `:cljd`-first; every JVM/Dart/Node branch follows the landed idioms (`.deleteSync` recursive catch-all, `RandomAccessFile.setLength`, `fs.truncateSync`); temp dirs live under `target/` with per-host cleanup in a `finally` on every leg, file backends tracked and closed. I verified ASCII and ≤80 columns on all five files by hand (the engineer's cljstyle gate was blocked; the orchestrator should still run `cljstyle check` at landing, as they asked). Cost: 18 tests, ~5.4 s JVM; the Node/Dart bill is ~19 temp directories with fsync-per-put — the same order as the JVM run — acceptable for the fast lanes.\n\n## Findings\n\n1. **`:clean?` is, on every reachable state, exactly `(not :poisoned?)`** — `src/cljc/yin/vm/ucf/authority.cljc:145` (set), `:219` (cleared, always together with `:poisoned? true`), consumed at `:206` beside the independent-looking `(not (:poisoned? s))` at `:207`; docstring `:189-197` presents two conditions. Severity: **minor** (no behavioral hole — it makes the predicate stricter, never looser). Nothing else writes either flag, so the flag adds no discriminating power, and the engineer's own mutation table is the proof: to get a failure they had to delete \"the clean **and** poison checks\" *together* — deleting the `(:clean? s)` check alone fails no test. Fix (docstring, one sentence, at landing): after \"cleared by poison\", add \"which today makes it equivalent to not-poisoned; it is kept as the hook a stricter rule would fill\". Dropping the flag entirely is also defensible; keeping it with the sentence is my recommendation, since the brief asked for a recorded flag. See ruling (a).\n2. **`exclusive-capable?` is an unlocked snapshot that trusts the declaration, not the lock** — `authority.cljc:198-210` reads `@(:state authority)` off-lock and reads the lock kind from data captured at open. An out-of-band `fs/unlock!` of the backend's lock or a `file/close!` of the backend (both reachable from the composition, and the matrix itself uses the first) leaves the answer true until the next transition poisons. Severity: **nit** (this is the right design — a cross-process lock cannot be cheaply probed, and the declaration is the contract — but the docstring's \"with a held directory lock\" overstates what was checked). Fix: one docstring sentence — \"the lock kind and failure model are the declaration the backend made at open, and the flags are read instantaneously; neither the lock nor the file is probed\".\n3. **`canon` normalization is applied to the five memory rows with fixed lease ids, where it is not needed** — `crash_cut_test.cljc:226-233, 334-346`. Severity: **nit**. For those rows the twin and the run use identical fixed ids, and the file leg already compares raw; unconditional renaming could in principle mask a mutation that respelled a lease id while keeping its grant-t and terms — which is content-free and also impossible without editing the table. No change required; noted so nobody reads the memory leg as byte-exact (the fixture test is where byte-exactness lives).\n4. **The fixture's file-backend leg pins only the header frame's hex among the reopened frames** — `ledger_fixture_test.cljc:118-124` (digest + first-frame hex). Severity: **nit**. The digest covers the folded content and the memory leg compares every frame's hex, but a frame-by-frame hex equality of the *replayed-through-dao.jing.file* frames against the pinned lines would also pin that `records` + `segment-bytes` round-trip the exact bytes rather than re-encodings that happen to fold alike (C2's finding 3 noted the re-encode check is defense in depth). Fix: one `(is (= (map lf/hex (:frames @pinned)) (map lf/hex ...)))` line at landing.\n5. **`:persisted #{:identity :content-references}` still has no authority-side semantics** — surfaced verbatim by `authority/durability` (`authority.cljc:174-182`) from `file.cljc:41`, documented only as a set in `dao.stream.journal.md:204`. The C2 review deferred this to \"C3 must pin the semantics when the ledger gives it meaning\"; C12 is where the authority first hands the declaration to compositions, and the pin is still owed — it matters now: the journal persists the *references* (offer variant addresses in the frames), never the checkpoint *content* (the store is the composition's `dao.jing` byte-store), so a reopen over an empty store is open and clean but inherited-id admissions answer `:suspended` (C9's unreadable checkpoint). Severity: **minor** (doc; sentence below).\n6. **`a-poisoned-file-authority-is-not-capable` abandons its header-landing authority value unclosed** — `durability_test.cljc:138`. The first `open!` exists only to write the header through the real seam so the armed cut fires on the enrollment, not on the header; its authority is then discarded. Harmless (the `finally` closes the backend and removes the directory; nothing reads the value). Severity: **nit**, no change required.\n\n## Rulings on the engineer's four questions\n\n**(a) Should `:clean?` also require that no tenure is left live at open (editing grant/reopen!)? No — accept the current definition, amended only by finding 1's sentence.** Exclusivity is a claim about *writers*: the directory lock bars every other process from the ledger, and the failure model says what survives — that is what `exclusive-capable?` gates. Tenures live at open are a *fencing* concern, and fencing is already served where it belongs: `grant/reopen!` reclaims them epoch-by-epoch, stale holders answer `:stale`, and a plain `open!` that leaves them live is a legitimate configuration (a lease granted by this same ledger genuinely persists; admitting under it is correct, not a violation). Tying an authority-core flag to the grant slice would also invert the layering — `authority.cljc` sits below `grant.cljc`, and reopen! already routes through `open!`. The stricter rule would forbid a clean-exclusive reader, which nothing in the plan or UCF asks for. Do not edit `grant.cljc`.\n\n**(b) Should the composition guard enrollment retries (enroll! has no request id)? Accept the engineer's resolution; owe one sentence, no src change.** The matrix's `enroll-once!` is the right discipline and the right shape: the target identity is derived deterministically (`ledger/target-identity arb t`), so the composition rereads the projection after an uncertain answer and enrolls only when that identity is absent. A *blind* retry is not idempotent — it enrolls a second target at the next t, and a driver that then admits to the new target splits effects across two streams — which is why the sentence must be written (below), in the restart/reopen material D reads. Adding a request id now would touch the enrolled fact's published bytes and the fold for a transition that is authority-authored, not a remote request; C11's front correctly carries no enroll request. Revisit only if a remote enrollment front ever appears.\n\n**(c) Should `grant/step!` return a reply of its own? No.** A judge-step is a state-threaded pass in dao.lease's forward-step shape; its observable outcome *is* the ledger's — `t` advanced or not, or a poisoned authority — and `step!` derives exactly that. A returned reply would be a second truth that can diverge from the committed one (the entire C12 premise is that the journal is the only authority). If D wants a reply, D derives it the same way, in one place; the row changes in one place then, as the engineer says.\n\n**(d) Should the `grant/reopen!` retry leg also run on the file backend? Not for C12 — accept; it is E's cheapest first row.** Every mechanism reopen! composes is already proven on real file frames by this suite: opening a dropped-handle, torn-tailed directory (the file leg's reopen) and committing lapse transactions through the file seam (the reclaim row). What is untested is only the composition, in-process — and the composition *with a real death in the middle* is precisely E's clause-10 row (\"crash cuts around completion and around result delivery\", UCF 7.11.1 clause 10), where a reopen over a really-killed process's directory is the natural first test. If the orchestrator wants it now anyway, the cheap shape is to run `grant/reopen!` on a fresh `file/backend!` *before* the plain retry and assert the `:reopened` column — a restructure of `run-file-cut`'s leg, not a new row; I do not require it.\n\n## Doc sentences C12 forces (listed, not written into the tree — the brief forbade doc edits)\n\n1. **Linker-dht 14.3 / UCF 7.7.8 area (the gate predicate, plan §5's \"durability declaration replaces a bare capability claim\"):** *An exclusive composition asks `authority/exclusive-capable?` for the failure model it requires; the answer is true only for a file backend whose declared lock kind is not `:none`, on an open, unpoisoned authority opened through `open!`/`grant/reopen!` with no poison since, whose declared failure model ranks at or above the requirement (`:none` < `:process-crash` < `:power-loss`). A memory backend is never capable. The lock kind and failure model are the declaration captured at open; neither the lock nor the file is probed live.*\n2. **Plan 1.8 correction:** the sentence \"Dart file backends declare `:process-crash`\" should read \"every host's file backend declares `:process-crash`\" (the directory is never synced on any host; `no-host-is-capable-against-power-loss` is now code-tested).\n3. **Enrollment retry discipline (UCF 7.7.7/7.7.8 *Restart* or dht 14.2, beside reopen):** *`enroll!` carries no request identity. After an uncertain answer the composition rereads the projection and enrolls only when the derived target identity is absent; a blind retry enrolls a second target.*\n4. **The `:persisted` pin (finding 5, `dao.stream.journal.md` beside the declaration table):** *`:identity` — the header's identity survives reopen; `:content-references` — the frames persist the addresses the ledger references (offer variants, reported results), never the content itself: the checkpoint store is the composition's byte-store, and a reopen over an empty store stays open and clean while inherited-id admissions answer `:suspended`.*\n5. **Cost paragraph (journal.md or the transactor-exception section):** *reopen is O(history) — every open replays every frame, re-encoding and re-hashing each record on the file backend, and folds every record, with nothing checkpointed; each transition is one fsync, and a reopen adds one per live tenure; `successor-seen?` costs O(occurrences) per report decision and per replayed edge; an inherited-id admission costs O(chain × occurrences) plus a content-store read, all under the authority lock; the whole ledger is in memory (plan §4 risk 5).*\n6. **The clause-10 boundary sentence (UCF 7.11.1 or the C12 report's landing note):** *C12 proves the substrate — every authority transition under every journal cut reopens to its pre-state or its post-state with only whole transactions and a converging retry, and one ledger's frames are byte-identical across hosts — it does not close clause 10: crash, partition and successor-completion through the wired composition, with real kills, remain stage E.*\n\nOne process note for the landing record: the engineer's cljstyle gate was permission-blocked; I verified ASCII and the 80-column limit on all five files by hand, and the orchestrator should still run `cljstyle check` with the three-host lanes.\n\n---\n\nRead-only review; no repository file edited and no suite run. The one permitted write was the reviewer plan file at `/Users/sto/.claude-glm/plans/read-users-sto-workspace-datomworld-c12g-lively-shamir.md`, which records this review's method.\n3:   their replay (UCF 7.7.5, 7.7.8, 7.11.1 fenced-custody clause 3;\n7:   stream read, an FFI result or a link result.  A request is\n10:      :yin.k/input-seq k :yin.k/source s :yin.k/observed v}\n14:   with kind one of `source-kinds`; `v` is the outcome as data, a gap\n25:   commit.  Equal content at a recorded k answers `:replayed`; other\n30:   a wrong epoch is `:stale`, even for a k already recorded.\n34:   Frontier.  A lease's frontier is the count of input records of its\n36:   replays records 0 to frontier - 1 in order (`inputs`,\n37:   `replay-input`), checks each source, and fails closed on a mismatch.\n38:   At the frontier the prefix has ended; live observation resumes and\n39:   is recorded from there.  The prefix never extends past the frontier,\n40:   so a holder's own later records are not replayed to it, and an empty\n41:   prefix (frontier 0) is distinct from missing evidence, which fails\n45:   `record-input!` answers `:recorded` or `:replayed`, or `replay-input`\n46:   answers `:replayed`.  The authority cannot see dependence: a driver\n55:(def source-kinds\n56:  \"The kinds of input a source names.\"\n57:  #{:yin.k/read :yin.k/ffi-result :yin.k/link-result})\n68:    :yin.k/source :yin.k/observed})\n71:(defn request\n73:   lease `l` at epoch `e` from `source`.\"\n74:  [o l e k source observed]\n79:   :yin.k/source source\n83:(defn- source?\n87:       (contains? source-kinds (:yin.k/kind s))\n91:(defn- portable?\n98:(defn- well-formed?\n106:       (source? (:yin.k/source r))\n111:(defn- status\n116:(defn- tenure\n134:(defn- content\n135:  [source observed]\n136:  {:yin.k/source source :yin.k/observed observed})\n139:(defn- decision\n145:           k :yin.k/input-seq s :yin.k/source v :yin.k/observed} r\n160:                (if (and (cbor/content= s (:yin.k/source rec))\n162:                  {:yin.k/status :replayed :yin.k/occurrence o\n166:                   :yin.k/recorded-input (content (:yin.k/source rec)\n178:                                 :yin.k/source s\n184:(defn record-input!\n191:     {:yin.k/status :replayed :yin.k/occurrence o :yin.k/input-seq k}\n200:   `{:yin.k/source s :yin.k/observed v}`) and :input-gap (carrying\n212:(defn frontier\n213:  \"The frontier of lease `l` in projection `p`: the count of input\n224:(defn inputs\n225:  \"The replay prefix of lease `l` in projection `p`, for a regranted\n228:     {:yin.k/occurrence o :dao.lease/lease l :yin.k/frontier n\n229:      :yin.k/inputs [{:yin.k/input-seq k :yin.k/source s\n242:      (let [n (frontier p l)]\n245:         :yin.k/frontier n\n251:                                        :yin.k/source (:yin.k/source rec)\n257:(defn replay-input\n258:  \"The k-th input for a holder replaying `prefix`, an `inputs` answer,\n259:   that asks it of `source`:\n261:     {:yin.k/status :replayed :yin.k/input-seq k :yin.k/observed v}\n262:     {:yin.k/status :refused :yin.k/reason :source-mismatch\n263:      :yin.k/input-seq k :yin.k/recorded-source s\n264:      :yin.k/observed-sour